  <h1>Statistics Math in Trading</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Why this matters for <strong>HFT_practice</strong>: <code>analyze_equity.py</code>
    is 40 lines that compute every headline number you'll ever quote for a strategy —
    CAGR, Sharpe, max drawdown, annualized vol. Each line encodes a statistical idea
    you need to defend. This lesson walks the math behind those lines so you can
    interpret the numbers honestly and write the next ones yourself.
  </p>

  <!-- ===================== §1 RETURNS ===================== -->
  <h2>1. Returns: simple vs log, and why both exist</h2>

  <p>
    Every metric in this lesson starts with <strong>returns</strong>, not prices.
    (Recall Lesson 0005: returns are roughly stationary; prices aren't.) Two
    standard definitions:
  </p>

  <div class="formula">
    r<sub>t</sub><sup>simple</sup> = (P<sub>t</sub> − P<sub>t−1</sub>) / P<sub>t−1</sub>  =  P<sub>t</sub>/P<sub>t−1</sub> − 1
    <span class="small-note">Simple return — what <code>pct_change()</code> computes</span>
  </div>

  <div class="formula">
    r<sub>t</sub><sup>log</sup> = ln(P<sub>t</sub> / P<sub>t−1</sub>)
    <span class="small-note">Log return — additive across time, used in academic papers and risk math</span>
  </div>

  <p>The two properties that make each useful:</p>

  <table>
    <tr><th></th><th>Simple return</th><th>Log return</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Compounds via</td><td><code>∏(1 + r)</code> &nbsp; (product)</td><td><code>∑ r</code> &nbsp; (sum)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Aggregates across <em>assets</em> linearly?</td><td><strong>Yes</strong> (portfolio return = Σ wᵢ rᵢ)</td><td>No</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Aggregates across <em>time</em> linearly?</td><td>No</td><td><strong>Yes</strong> (multi-day log return = sum of daily log returns)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Symmetric around 0?</td><td>No (−50% / +100% ≠ same magnitude)</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
  </table>

  <p>
    Both columns are right, in different contexts. This repo uses <strong>simple
    returns</strong> throughout (<code>pct_change()</code> everywhere) because the
    main operation is portfolio aggregation: <em>"how did my equally-weighted top-N
    do today?"</em> Portfolio math is cleaner with simple returns.
  </p>

  <div class="callout">
    <strong>The one identity that ties them together:</strong>
    <code>(1 + r<sup>simple</sup>) = exp(r<sup>log</sup>)</code>. For small returns
    (|r| &lt; 5%) they are numerically nearly identical. They diverge fast for
    crypto-sized moves; equities are usually fine.
  </div>

  <!-- ===================== §2 VOL & SHARPE ===================== -->
  <h2>2. Volatility, the Sharpe ratio, and the annualization factor</h2>

  <p>
    <strong>Volatility</strong> in finance just means "standard deviation of returns."
    By itself, that number is daily — small and unhelpful for comparison across
    horizons. So we always annualize:
  </p>

  <div class="formula">
    σ<sub>ann</sub> = σ<sub>daily</sub> × √252
    <span class="small-note">252 ≈ number of US trading days per year</span>
  </div>

  <h3>Why √252 and not 252?</h3>
  <p>
    If daily returns are independent with mean μ and variance σ², then over N days
    the sum has mean Nμ and variance Nσ². <strong>Means add; variances add.</strong>
    Annualized volatility is the standard deviation of the <em>annual</em> return,
    so you take the square root of the summed variance: √(252·σ²) = σ·√252. This
    is the central limit theorem doing its job.
  </p>
  <p class="small">
    The assumption of independence is wrong in detail (returns have autocorrelation,
    volatility clusters), but √T scaling is the universal first-pass convention and
    every reported "annualized vol" uses it.
  </p>

  <h3>The Sharpe ratio</h3>
  <p>
    A unit-free measure of return per unit of risk. The textbook version uses
    excess return over a risk-free rate; this repo uses the simpler "raw"
    version (assumes risk-free = 0):
  </p>

  <div class="formula">
    Sharpe = (mean daily return × 252) / σ<sub>ann</sub>
    <span class="small-note">Equivalent to: mean daily / σ<sub>daily</sub> × √252</span>
  </div>

  <p>
    A Sharpe of 1.0 means "per unit of annual standard deviation, you earn one unit
    of annual mean." Industry rough guide: &lt;0.5 mediocre, 1.0 solid, 2.0
    excellent, 3.0+ raise eyebrows (or fees). HFT strategies routinely exceed
    Sharpe 5 because their bets are many and small.
  </p>

  <h3>Tiny worked example</h3>
<pre><span class="kw">import</span> numpy <span class="kw">as</span> np
<span class="kw">import</span> math

<span class="com"># 252 daily returns, mean 0.05% per day, std 1% per day</span>
ret = np.random.<span class="fn">normal</span>(<span class="num">0.0005</span>, <span class="num">0.01</span>, <span class="num">252</span>)

mean_daily  = ret.<span class="fn">mean</span>()
std_daily   = ret.<span class="fn">std</span>()
mean_ann    = mean_daily * <span class="num">252</span>            <span class="com"># ~12.6%   — means scale linearly</span>
vol_ann     = std_daily  * math.<span class="fn">sqrt</span>(<span class="num">252</span>)  <span class="com"># ~15.9%   — std scales by sqrt(T)</span>
sharpe      = mean_ann / vol_ann               <span class="com"># ~0.79</span>
</pre>

  <!-- ===================== §3 DRAWDOWN & CAGR ===================== -->
  <h2>3. Drawdown and CAGR</h2>

  <h3>Maximum drawdown — the worst the curve ever felt</h3>
  <p>
    <strong>Drawdown</strong> at time t is the percentage drop from the running
    peak so far:
  </p>

  <div class="formula">
    DD<sub>t</sub> = equity<sub>t</sub> / max<sub>s≤t</sub>(equity<sub>s</sub>) − 1
    <span class="small-note">Always ≤ 0. The minimum over the whole period is the "max drawdown."</span>
  </div>

  <p>
    In code this is two lines: a running cumulative max, then a ratio:
  </p>

<pre>cum      = (<span class="num">1</span> + ret).<span class="fn">cumprod</span>()       <span class="com"># 1 + cumulative growth path</span>
roll_max = cum.<span class="fn">cummax</span>()                <span class="com"># running peak so far</span>
maxdd    = (cum / roll_max - <span class="num">1</span>).<span class="fn">min</span>()   <span class="com"># worst drop from peak</span>
</pre>

  <p>
    Why care: Sharpe says nothing about <em>shape</em>. Two strategies can have
    identical Sharpe and very different max drawdowns. Drawdown is the number
    investors actually pull capital over.
  </p>

  <h3>CAGR — geometric annual growth, the honest version of "average return"</h3>
  <p>
    Two ways to summarize "what return did I earn per year on average":
  </p>

  <div class="formula">
    APR<sub>simple</sub> = mean(daily return) × 252
    <span class="small-note">Arithmetic — ignores compounding</span>
  </div>

  <div class="formula">
    CAGR = (final / initial)<sup>252/N</sup> − 1
    <span class="small-note">Geometric — what actually compounded to that final value</span>
  </div>

  <p>
    They are <strong>not</strong> the same number. CAGR &lt; APR<sub>simple</sub>
    by an amount that grows with volatility (this is the
    <em>volatility drag</em>: <code>CAGR ≈ μ − σ²/2</code> in log terms). A
    strategy with daily returns alternating +10% / −10% has APR ≈ 0 but
    CAGR ≈ −1.005% per period — because two such days compound to
    1.10 × 0.90 = 0.99, not 1.00. Real money cares about CAGR.
  </p>

  <!-- ===================== §4 COV & CUSUM ===================== -->
  <h2>4. Covariance matrix and the CUSUM statistic</h2>

  <h3>Covariance — when assets move together</h3>
  <p>
    Variance generalizes to <strong>covariance</strong> when you have multiple
    assets:
  </p>

  <div class="formula">
    Σ<sub>ij</sub> = E[(r<sub>i</sub> − μ<sub>i</sub>)(r<sub>j</sub> − μ<sub>j</sub>)]
    <span class="small-note">Σ<sub>ii</sub> = variance of asset i. Σ<sub>ij</sub> = how i and j co-move.</span>
  </div>

  <p>
    For a portfolio with weight vector <code>w</code>:
  </p>

  <div class="formula">
    portfolio variance = w<sup>T</sup> Σ w
  </div>

  <p>
    That single equation is why correlations matter: two highly-correlated
    assets in a portfolio don't diversify you, no matter how good each one is
    individually. The mean-variance optimizer (Lesson 0007's target) lives or
    dies on a stable estimate of Σ. The repo estimates it as the sample covariance
    of recent daily returns over a <code>--cov_lookback</code> window (default 90 days).
  </p>

  <h3>CUSUM — detecting a regime break</h3>
  <p>
    The <strong>cumulative sum (CUSUM)</strong> test detects when a time series
    has drifted away from its mean — i.e. when something structurally changed.
    Two running sums track upward and downward drift:
  </p>

  <div class="formula">
    S<sup>+</sup><sub>t</sub> = max(0, S<sup>+</sup><sub>t−1</sub> + (r<sub>t</sub> − μ) − k)
    <br>
    S<sup>−</sup><sub>t</sub> = min(0, S<sup>−</sup><sub>t−1</sub> + (r<sub>t</sub> − μ) + k)
    <span class="small-note">k is a "slack" parameter, typically a multiple of σ. Trigger when |S| crosses a threshold.</span>
  </div>

  <p>
    The repo uses CUSUM as one of four stress detectors in
    <code>walkforward_sim.py</code>, with <code>k = cusum_threshold × σ</code> where
    <code>cusum_threshold</code> defaults to 3. The intuition: small noise should
    leave both running sums near zero; a sustained drift in either direction
    pushes one of them past the threshold and signals a regime change.
  </p>

  <!-- ===================== §5 IN THIS PROJECT ===================== -->
  <h2>5. In this project</h2>

  <p>
    Time to read the actual code. Every formula above lives in roughly 20 lines of
    <code>analyze_equity.py</code>.
  </p>

  <span class="filepath">analyze_equity.py:compute_metrics (lines 7–40)</span>
<pre><span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">compute_metrics</span>(eq: pd.Series) -> dict:
    eq = eq.<span class="fn">dropna</span>()
    initial, final = <span class="fn">float</span>(eq.iloc[<span class="num">0</span>]), <span class="fn">float</span>(eq.iloc[-<span class="num">1</span>])
    total_return = final / initial - <span class="num">1.0</span>          <span class="com"># cumulative return over the period</span>

