  <!-- ===================== §6 OFTEN CONFUSED WITH ===================== -->
  <h2>6. Often confused with</h2>

  <div class="grid2">
    <div class="card">
      <h4>CAGR vs APR (arithmetic mean × 252)</h4>
      <p>
        <strong>CAGR</strong> = geometric — what actually compounded.
        <strong>APR<sub>simple</sub></strong> = mean × 252 — what arithmetic says.
        APR is always ≥ CAGR. The gap is <em>volatility drag</em> ≈ σ²/2.
        Quote CAGR to investors; quote both internally.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>Volatility vs Variance</h4>
      <p>
        <strong>Variance</strong> = E[(r − μ)²]. Units: returns².
        <strong>Volatility</strong> = √variance. Units: returns. People say "vol"
        when they want a number they can compare to mean return (same units).
        Variance is what mathematically adds across time; vol is what gets quoted.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>Sharpe vs Sortino</h4>
      <p>
        <strong>Sharpe</strong> divides excess return by total standard deviation.
        <strong>Sortino</strong> divides by <em>downside</em> deviation only
        (treats upside vol as good). Sortino is kinder to strategies with
        positively skewed returns; this repo computes only Sharpe.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>Drawdown vs Loss</h4>
      <p>
        A <strong>loss</strong> is realized P&L below your starting capital.
        A <strong>drawdown</strong> is below your running <em>peak</em> — you can
        have a 30% drawdown while still up 50% from your start. Investors
        emotionally treat both as failures; drawdown is the harsher measure.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>Sample std (<code>ddof=1</code>) vs Population std (<code>ddof=0</code>)</h4>
      <p>
        Sample uses (n−1) in the denominator (unbiased estimator from a sample).
        Population uses n (when you have the whole population). For finite return
        samples we want ddof=1. pandas defaults to 1; numpy defaults to 0.
        Same word, different number.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>Correlation vs Covariance</h4>
      <p>
        <strong>Covariance</strong> has the units of the underlying variables
        (returns²) and depends on their scale.
        <strong>Correlation</strong> = covariance / (σᵢ σⱼ) is unit-free and
        bounded in [−1, +1]. Use correlation to compare assets; use covariance
        in the portfolio variance formula.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

