  <!-- ===================== §8 SCENARIOS ===================== -->
  <h2>8. Check your understanding (scenarios)</h2>

  <details class="scenario">
    <summary>S1. A teammate ports <code>compute_metrics</code> to NumPy and now Vol comes back ~0.5% lower than the original. What's the most likely cause?</summary>
    <p>
      The <code>std()</code> default. pandas uses <code>ddof=1</code> (sample std,
      divides by n−1); NumPy uses <code>ddof=0</code> (population std, divides
      by n). For ~252 returns, n vs n−1 is a ~0.2% difference in the variance,
      ~0.1% in the std, which scales to ~0.1 × √252 ≈ 1.5% in annualized vol —
      easily explains the gap. Fix: pass <code>ddof=1</code> explicitly when
      calling NumPy. Don't trust same-named functions across libraries.
    </p>
  </details>

  <details class="scenario">
    <summary>S2. You add the constraint that risk-free rate is 4% annual (T-bill yield). How does the Sharpe formula in <code>analyze_equity.py</code> change?</summary>
    <p>
      The textbook Sharpe is <em>excess</em> return over the risk-free rate divided
      by the std of <em>excess</em> returns. The cleanest patch:
      <code>excess = ret - rf_daily</code> where <code>rf_daily = 0.04 / 252</code>,
      then <code>sharpe = (excess.mean() * 252) / (excess.std() * sqrt(252))</code>.
      In practice the denominator barely changes because rf is nearly constant, so
      most quick implementations subtract rf only in the numerator. Either is
      defensible; just be consistent and disclose which one you used.
    </p>
  </details>

  <details class="scenario">
    <summary>S3. Two strategies both report Sharpe = 1.5 over the same period. One has MaxDD = 12%, the other MaxDD = 45%. Which would you allocate to, and why doesn't Sharpe alone tell you?</summary>
    <p>
      The one with MaxDD = 12% — and Sharpe can't see the difference because it
      measures the average tradeoff between mean and std, not the shape of the
      worst stretch. Two return series can have identical mean and std (hence
      identical Sharpe) and very different drawdown profiles, especially if one
      has fat negative tails or path-dependent losses. This is exactly why this
      repo computes drawdown alongside Sharpe — and why investors actually live
      with the drawdown number, not the Sharpe number.
    </p>
  </details>

